  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Warmer and increasing humidity today, with a chance \nfor a few showers and thunderstorms. \n\nAny river valley fog that does form overnight will dissipate quickly \nafter sunrise this morning. After that, we should see temperatures \nrapidly warm as we march through the morning hours. Expect a range \nof 80s across the region, with a few spots even touching 90F in the \nGenesee Valley during peak heating. We will also see an increase in \nhumidity levels, this as moisture gets fed up around the western \nperiphery of the Bermuda high. The combination of warming temps and \nincreasing moisture will place heat indexes in the upper 80s to low \n90s in the warmest spots this afternoon. \n\nWith increasing moisture we could also see a few afternoon-eve \nshowers and thunderstorms. Given PW values ramping up to 1.5-1.7 \ninches any storms that do form could produce locally heavy down \npours. The best chance to see a storm will be across the western \nSouthern Tier and Genesee Valley, with lesser chances for the \neastern Lake Ontario region. A few showers may linger into this \nevening and tonight but should weaken with the loss of daytime \nheating. \n  \nKey Message 2...The chance for showers and thunderstorms increases \nThursday and Friday. Any stronger storms may produce locally heavy \ndownpours.  \n\nA shortwave trough and approaching front may bring better coverage \nof showers and thunderstorms on Thursday, with another round on \nFriday. Guidance shows MLCAPE values of +1500 J/kg Thursday but \nrelatively weak shear values (20-30 knots) which 'may' limit the \nsevere weather potential. That said...the main threat at this point \nappears to be localized heavy rainfall. WPC currently has placed all \nof New York in a Marginal Risk for excessive rainfall Thursday and \nFriday.",
